This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In the 50 years since The Power Broker was published, the book has endured in ways that few biographies have. First and foremost, it completely upended how the public viewed the former New York City Parks Commissioner Robert Moses. He went from being the man who built all those nice parks to an urban design villain. If you've been following along with the 99%

2
00:00:25.000 --> 00:01:02.000
visible breakdown of the Power Broker, you know what I'm talking about. And you also know that last month I was at the New York Historical Society with my co-host Elliot Kalin to interview Robert Caro, the author of the Power Broker, live on stage. The New York Historical Society holds Robert Caro's archives, which include his research for the Power Broker, as well as the papers for his four-volume biography of President Lyndon Johnson. At 89 years old, Caro is still working on the fifth and final installment. To celebrate 50 years of the Power Broker, the Society went into their archives and curated

